# Rank 1708 / CVE-2026-22043: RustFS's policy engine evaluates
# `deny_only` requests by skipping the Allow pass and returning allowed.
# `Policy::is_allowed` (crates/policy/src/policy/policy.rs:66 at 356dc7e^)
# runs the Deny statements and then reads
# `if args.deny_only || args.is_owner { return true; }`, so any caller that
# asks for a deny-only evaluation is granted access with no matching Allow
# statement. Three admin handlers reach that path on a self-service
# condition rather than on a permission: AddServiceAccount
# (rustfs/src/admin/handlers/service_account.rs:115) derives the flag from
# `constant_time_eq(&cred.access_key, &target_user) ||
# constant_time_eq(&cred.parent_user, &target_user)`, and AddUser and
# GetUserInfo (rustfs/src/admin/handlers/user.rs:121, :378) from
# `ak == cred.access_key`. A restricted service account whose parent matches
# the target therefore passes the gate without holding
# CreateServiceAccountAdminAction / CreateUserAdminAction and mints an
# unrestricted credential (CWE-269, reported by Positive Technologies as
# PT-2026-2144).
#
# The row names 356dc7e0c2b4438ef3b447160003ea4571fb99ad ("feat: Add
# permission verification for account creation", #1401): `is_allowed` splits
# the disjunction so deny-only mode returns false, and the three call sites
# pass a literal `false` / `deny_only: false` instead of the identity
# comparison. The pool row originally carried
# a95e549430851f3c34ee4cc8dd7be60aa200e8c4, the peeled commit of the
# 1.0.0-alpha.79 release tag the advisory names, which is an audit-registry
# and TCP-keepalive cleanup whose parent is already fully patched
# (356dc7e is an ancestor five commits earlier); the row has since been
# repointed to 356dc7e. The vulnerable construct is present at 356dc7e^ and
# the remediation at 356dc7e.
#
# ATTENTION, rechecked: the wiring-not-dataflow family (the
# 1514/1535/1540/1556/1557/1593 shape, "a flag defaulting open"). The defect
# is that a permission check is skipped, so no tainted value reaches a sink
# and no taint rule can see it: the request body's target_user is labelled
# code.ObjectLookupByUserInput at the get_user lookup, and
# new_service_account/create_user have no sink binding, but the escalation
# happens in the gate's return value, not in any data that moves.
#
# Re-measured on the real checkout over three --cache off runs per revision
# (`timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB -flags with` over
# 616 rust + 36 config + 714 textpattern files): 609 findings and 1824
# review flags on every one of the six runs, and the combined multisets are
# byte-identical between the vulnerable and the fixed revision once line
# numbers are normalized (diff 0; the only raw differences are the line
# shifts from the fix's added test function and comments). The findings
# total has grown from the 591 of the first pass, so vocabulary shipped
# since then does fire on this tree -- identically at both revisions, so
# none of it discriminates. No finding lands on policy.rs,
# service_account.rs or user.rs; the flags there are IndexAccess,
# ResourceReview over read_to_end, and ObjectLookupByUserInput -- nothing
# about the gate. The full `-flags with` view was used, so this is a clean
# zero at both revisions, not a misread one.
#
# Both discriminators were re-probed with the current engine and neither is
# a detector:
#
#   * Callee shape (`node.context.binary contains "deny_only"` plus the
#     `is_allowed` call token): `is_allowed` itself drops out at the fixed
#     revision -- the split `if`s leave no binary mentioning the field --
#     but the shape also fires on the safe central authorize_request path
#     (rustfs/src/storage/access.rs:39) at BOTH revisions, where `deny_only`
#     appears only as the literal-false argument spelling inside the Args
#     literals. Beyond that it names the vendor's own field and encodes the
#     fixing commit's layout, the two shapes the review rejects.
#   * Caller shape (`node.context.binary contains "access_key"` plus a gate
#     call token): 12 functions at the vulnerable revision, 10 at the fixed.
#     AddUser::call and GetUserInfo::call drop out, but
#     AddServiceAccount::call matches at both revisions through an
#     unrelated `target_user != cred.access_key` comparison that survives
#     the fix, and RemoveUser::call and ImportIam::call match at both
#     revisions with the opposite polarity -- their identity comparisons
#     REFUSE the operation ("can't remove self", a `ak ==
#     sys_cred.access_key || ... || cred.parent_user == ak` guard) and
#     their gates pass a literal false. The same tokens carry both the
#     vulnerable and the safe meaning, so no function-scoped fact separates
#     them.
#   * `deny_only` occurs in rustfs alone across the 264 target clones under
#     /tmp/cve-work, so a binding keyed on it can never fire on unrelated
#     code and fails the genericity test; the precise caller variant needs
#     access_key/parent_user/target_user/constant_time_eq equally.
#   * Rust still emits no `assign:` token for any `let` form (re-probed:
#     literal, `==`, call and `||` right-hand sides all match zero
#     functions), so the derivation from the identity comparison to the
#     gate argument is not linkable; assignment-through-temporary stores do
#     emit one. This is not the blocker -- with the token, both shapes
#     above still fail precision or genericity as measured.
#
# No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry: nothing here is a localized engine construct
# mishandled -- the token routes the caller shape needs do exist (`call:`,
# `binary:`, the body token), and what is missing is a language-neutral
# concept for "an authorization gate relaxed by an actor/target identity
# match" whose polarity is readable from the code, which is ontology design;
# every spelling that fits one repository names that repository. No
# definition change shipped. Spec cve_rank1708 records the engine's
# deny-only grant and the self-service caller, both forms, each asserting
# reject VYQL-AUTH-080: when incorrect-authorization vocabulary gains a
# Rust emitter for a gate whose permissive mode is caller-derived, the
# vulnerable forms must flip to expect.

test "rank1708: the policy engine grants access from deny-only mode"
  lang rust
  file crates/policy/src/policy/policy.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub enum Effect { Allow, Deny }

  pub struct Statement {
      pub effect: Effect,
      pub action: String,
  }

  pub struct Policy {
      pub statements: Vec<Statement>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  impl Policy {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Deny) && !statement.matches(args).await {
                  return false;
              }
          }

          if args.deny_only || args.is_owner {
              return true;
          }

          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Allow) && statement.matches(args).await {
                  return true;
              }
          }

          false
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1708: the fixed revision returns denied from deny-only mode"
  lang rust
  file crates/policy/src/policy/policy.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub enum Effect { Allow, Deny }

  pub struct Statement {
      pub effect: Effect,
      pub action: String,
  }

  pub struct Policy {
      pub statements: Vec<Statement>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  impl Policy {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          // First, check all Deny statements - if any Deny matches, deny the request
          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Deny) && !statement.matches(args).await {
                  return false;
              }
          }

          // Owner has all permissions
          if args.is_owner {
              return true;
          }

          if args.deny_only {
              return false;
          }

          // Check Allow statements
          for statement in self.statements.iter() {
              if matches!(statement.effect, Effect::Allow) && statement.matches(args).await {
                  return true;
              }
          }

          false
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1708: a service account gate relaxed by an actor/target identity match"
  lang rust
  file rustfs/src/admin/handlers/service_account.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub struct Credentials {
      pub access_key: String,
      pub parent_user: String,
      pub claims: Option<HashMap<String, String>>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  pub struct IamStore;

  impl IamStore {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          let _ = args;
          true
      }

      pub async fn new_service_account(&self, target_user: &str, opts: &str) -> Credentials {
          Credentials { access_key: target_user.to_string(), parent_user: String::new(), claims: None }
      }
  }

  fn constant_time_eq(a: &str, b: &str) -> bool {
      a.as_bytes() == b.as_bytes()
  }

  pub async fn add_service_account(cred: &Credentials, target_user: &str, owner: bool, iam_store: &IamStore) {
      let deny_only = constant_time_eq(&cred.access_key, target_user) || constant_time_eq(&cred.parent_user, target_user);

      if !iam_store
          .is_allowed(&Args {
              account: &cred.access_key,
              action: "CreateServiceAccountAdminAction",
              is_owner: owner,
              claims: &HashMap::new(),
              deny_only,
          })
          .await
      {
          return;
      }

      let opts = "unrestricted";
      let _ = iam_store.new_service_account(target_user, opts).await;
  }
  ```

test "rank1708: the fixed revision always requires the explicit Allow permission"
  lang rust
  file rustfs/src/admin/handlers/service_account.rs
  reject VYQL-AUTH-080
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashMap;

  pub struct Credentials {
      pub access_key: String,
      pub parent_user: String,
      pub claims: Option<HashMap<String, String>>,
  }

  pub struct Args<'a> {
      pub account: &'a str,
      pub action: &'a str,
      pub is_owner: bool,
      pub deny_only: bool,
      pub claims: &'a HashMap<String, String>,
  }

  pub struct IamStore;

  impl IamStore {
      pub async fn is_allowed(&self, args: &Args<'_>) -> bool {
          let _ = args;
          true
      }

      pub async fn new_service_account(&self, target_user: &str, opts: &str) -> Credentials {
          Credentials { access_key: target_user.to_string(), parent_user: String::new(), claims: None }
      }
  }

  pub async fn add_service_account(cred: &Credentials, target_user: &str, owner: bool, iam_store: &IamStore) {
      // Always require explicit Allow permission
      if !iam_store
          .is_allowed(&Args {
              account: &cred.access_key,
              action: "CreateServiceAccountAdminAction",
              is_owner: owner,
              claims: &HashMap::new(),
              deny_only: false,
          })
          .await
      {
          return;
      }

      let opts = "unrestricted";
      let _ = iam_store.new_service_account(target_user, opts).await;
  }
  ```
